--TEST--
Every `Core\Process\Result` member answers the same thing on a second call — a captured stream is a value the result holds, not a pipe that drains, and all three members are asserted to agree with themselves — `rule:core-classes/process-run`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Process\Result::exitCode
// covers: Core\Process\Result::stdout
// covers: Core\Process\Result::stderr
// One question asked of all three members, so that a member which grew its own
// answer fails here while still reading correctly on its own line — the
// *agreement* shape, over the rule `rule:core-classes/process-run` states once for the whole
// class: `run` waits for the child, drains both pipes and builds a result out
// of what it read, so nothing the child owned is still open by the time a
// program has a result at all.
//
// What that rules out is the shape every previous generation of this API had.
// `exec` filled an array once, `popen` handed back a handle that emptied as it
// was read, and a second read of either answered something different from the
// first — so "read it once and keep it yourself" was a rule callers carried
// rather than a property the value had. Each member here is called twice with
// nothing in between and the two answers compared, which is the only way that
// difference is observable from a program.
//
// The first answer is pinned as well as compared. Two empty captures agree
// perfectly, and so do two zeroes: a member that drained on first read would
// pass an agreement test that only asked whether the answers matched. So each
// half asserts the marker is there *and* that the second call still says so,
// and the status is a number nothing but the child can have chosen.
//
// The sentinels differ from each other on purpose — a run in which no child
// started reports every member as disagreeing rather than as silently absent.
//
// The child is the platform's own shell, named absolutely, for the reason
// `process-a-completed-run-answers-the-status-the-child-chose.nvst` states.

bytes $firstOut = "sentinel one" as bytes;
bytes $secondOut = "sentinel two" as bytes;
bytes $firstErr = "sentinel one" as bytes;
bytes $secondErr = "sentinel two" as bytes;
int $firstCode = -2;
int $secondCode = -3;
int $started = 0;

try {
    var $unix = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", ["-c", "printf OUTPUT; printf DIAGNOSTIC 1>&2; exit 7"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $firstOut = $unix->stdout();
    $secondOut = $unix->stdout();
    $firstErr = $unix->stderr();
    $secondErr = $unix->stderr();
    $firstCode = $unix->exitCode();
    $secondCode = $unix->exitCode();
} catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
}

try {
    var $windows = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "echo OUTPUT& echo DIAGNOSTIC 1>&2& exit 7"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $firstOut = $windows->stdout();
    $secondOut = $windows->stdout();
    $firstErr = $windows->stderr();
    $secondErr = $windows->stderr();
    $firstCode = $windows->exitCode();
    $secondCode = $windows->exitCode();
} catch (IOError $notWindows) {
}

echo "started=", $started, "\n";
echo "exit=", $firstCode, "\n";

if ($firstCode == $secondCode) {
    echo "exitCode agrees with itself\n";
} else {
    echo "exitCode changed on a second call\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($firstOut, "OUTPUT" as bytes)) {
    echo "stdout carries the marker\n";
} else {
    echo "stdout lost the marker\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::compare($firstOut, $secondOut) == 0) {
    echo "stdout agrees with itself\n";
} else {
    echo "stdout changed on a second call\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($firstErr, "DIAGNOSTIC" as bytes)) {
    echo "stderr carries the marker\n";
} else {
    echo "stderr lost the marker\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::compare($firstErr, $secondErr) == 0) {
    echo "stderr agrees with itself\n";
} else {
    echo "stderr changed on a second call\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true` for the reason the exit-code case gives: the door's own scoping is
# pinned by `process-run-asks-for-the-capability-before-it-looks-at-the-target.nvst`,
# and this case is about what a result keeps after the child is gone.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
started=1
exit=7
exitCode agrees with itself
stdout carries the marker
stdout agrees with itself
stderr carries the marker
stderr agrees with itself
